      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report and literature review."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This article reports 2 cases of clay-shoveler's fracture equivalent in children presenting acutely after participation in sports."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "The clay-shoveler's fracture in adults is an avulsion fracture of the lower cervical or upper thoracic spinous process. To our knowledge, this is the first report in English literature on soft-tissue avulsion injury of the spinous process in children presenting with history and symptoms similar to clay-shoveler's fractures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Retrospective review of 2 cases."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A 14-year-old baseball player and a 16-year-old wrestler experienced acute posterior neck pain after participation in sports. Both patients presented with a history and physical examination suggestive of clay-shoveler's fracture but showed no evidence of injury on radiographs. Subsequent magnetic resonance images demonstrated an acute soft-tissue avulsion of the spinous process at C7 in 1 patient and T2 in the other. With nonoperative therapy, both patients returned to sports by 4 months, with occasional, intermittent discomfort a year after injury, which did not limit any activities."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In adolescents, if the history and physical examination are consistent with a clay-shoveler's fracture, but radiographs are normal, magnetic resonance imaging may be indicated to diagnose a soft-tissue avulsion."
        }
      ]},
